Here's a clean, idiomatic Go implementation. I'll provide both a **basic version** (exact match, case-sensitive) and a **robust version** (ignores case, spaces, and punctuation), since real-world palindrome checks often need the latter.

### 🔹 Basic Version (Case-Sensitive, Exact Match)
```go
package main

import "fmt"

// IsPalindrome checks if a string reads the same forwards and backwards.
// Case-sensitive and includes all characters.
func IsPalindrome(s string) bool {
	runes := []rune(s) // Convert to runes for proper Unicode support
	left, right := 0, len(runes)-1

	for left < right {
		if runes[left] != runes[right] {
			return false
		}
		left++
		right--
	}
	return true
}
```

### 🔹 Robust Version (Ignores Case, Spaces & Punctuation)
```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"unicode"
)

// IsPalindromeRobust checks if a string is a palindrome, ignoring
// case, spaces, and non-alphanumeric characters.
func IsPalindromeRobust(s string) bool {
	runes := []rune(s)
	left, right := 0, len(runes)-1

	for left < right {
		// Skip non-alphanumeric from the left
		for left < right && !unicode.IsLetter(runes[left]) && !unicode.IsDigit(runes[left]) {
			left++
		}
		// Skip non-alphanumeric from the right
		for left < right && !unicode.IsLetter(runes[right]) && !unicode.IsDigit(runes[right]) {
			right--
		}

		if left < right {
			if unicode.ToLower(runes[left]) != unicode.ToLower(runes[right]) {
				return false
			}
			left++
			right--
		}
	}
	return true
}
```

### 🧪 Complete Runnable Example
```go
func main() {
	fmt.Println("=== Basic ===")
	fmt.Println(IsPalindrome("racecar"))           // true
	fmt.Println(IsPalindrome("Racecar"))           // false (case-sensitive)
	fmt.Println(IsPalindrome("hello"))             // false
	fmt.Println(IsPalindrome(""))                  // true
	fmt.Println(IsPalindrome("🌍🌍"))              // true (Unicode-safe)

	fmt.Println("\n=== Robust ===")
	fmt.Println(IsPalindromeRobust("A man, a plan, a canal: Panama")) // true
	fmt.Println(IsPalindromeRobust("No 'x' in Nixon"))                // true
	fmt.Println(IsPalindromeRobust("hello world"))                    // false
}
```

### 📊 Complexity
- **Time:** `O(n)` where `n` is the length of the string. Each character is visited at most once.
- **Space:** `O(n)` for the `[]rune` conversion. This is necessary in Go to correctly handle multi-byte Unicode characters (emojis, accented letters, etc.). If you're certain the input is ASCII-only, you could use `[]byte(s)` for `O(1)` auxiliary space.

### 💡 Why `[]rune` instead of `[]byte`?
Go strings are UTF-8 encoded. A single character can take 1-4 bytes. Converting to `[]rune` gives you one element per Unicode code point, ensuring correct palindrome checks for international text and emojis.

